/-
# Problem Description

Erdős Problem 135: if every four points of a finite planar set determine at least five
distinct distances, must the set of `n` points determine `≫ n²` distinct distances?
`erdos_135` proves this is false.

Disproved by Tao, who constructed arbitrarily large point sets with the four-point
property whose number of distinct distances is `O(n² / sqrt(log n))`, which is `o(n²)`.

`HasPhi45 S` is the property `Φ(4,5)`: every four-element subset of `S` determines at
least five distances, i.e. `S` has no four-point subset with `distanceCount ≤ 4`. The
witness is a family `A : ℕ → Finset Plane` with `(A n).card = n` and `HasPhi45 (A n)` for
every `n`, whose distance counts are `=O[atTop] (fun n => (n : ℝ) ^ 2 / sqrt (log n))`.
-/
universe u v

